# 211. Design Add and Search Words Data Structure

**Problem Link:** [LeetCode – Design Add and Search Words Data Structure](https://leetcode.com/problems/design-add-and-search-words-data-structure/)

## Approach: Trie + Recursion + Backtracking

A Trie is a tree-based data structure used to store and search words efficiently.

Each Trie node contains:
- `isEndOfWord`: Indicates whether a complete word ends at the current node.
- `children[26]`: Stores pointers to the 26 lowercase English alphabet characters.

### Operations

1. **AddWord:** Traverse each character of the word. If the corresponding child node does not exist, create a new node. Mark the last node as the end of the word.

2. **Search:** Use a recursive function to traverse the Trie character by character.
   - If the character is a normal alphabet, move to its corresponding child node.
   - If the character is `.`, explore all available child nodes recursively.
   - When the entire word is traversed, check whether the current node marks the end of a complete word.

3. **Backtracking:** Whenever `.` is encountered, explore all possible character paths. If any path successfully matches the complete word, return `true`; otherwise, return `false`.

## Code

```cpp
class WordDictionary {
public:
    struct trieNode {
        bool isEndOfWord;
        trieNode* children[26];
    };

    trieNode* getNode() {
        trieNode* newNode = new trieNode();
        newNode->isEndOfWord = false;

        for(int i = 0; i < 26; i++)
            newNode->children[i] = NULL;

        return newNode;
    }

    trieNode* root;

    bool check(string& word, int index, trieNode* node) {
        if(index == word.length())
            return node->isEndOfWord;

        char ch = word[index];

        if(ch == '.') {
            for(int i = 0; i < 26; i++) {
                if(node->children[i] != NULL) {
                    if(check(word, index + 1, node->children[i]))
                        return true;
                }
            }
            return false;
        }

        int idx = ch - 'a';

        if(node->children[idx] == NULL)
            return false;

        return check(word, index + 1, node->children[idx]);
    }

    WordDictionary() {
        root = getNode();
    }

    void addWord(string word) {
        trieNode* node = root;

        for(int i = 0; i < word.length(); i++) {
            int idx = word[i] - 'a';

            if(node->children[idx] == NULL) {
                node->children[idx] = getNode();
            }

            node = node->children[idx];
        }

        node->isEndOfWord = true;
    }

    bool search(string word) {
        trieNode* node = root;
        return check(word, 0, node);
    }
};
```

## Complexity Analysis

Let:
- \(L\) = Length of the word being searched.
- \(N\) = Total number of nodes in the Trie.

| Operation | Time Complexity |
|---|---|
| AddWord | \(O(L)\) |
| Search | \(O(26^L)\) worst case |

**Space Complexity:** \(O(26N)\) for storing the Trie.

The recursive search requires an additional \(O(L)\) stack space in the worst case.

## Key Takeaway

Trie efficiently handles word insertion and searching. The `.` wildcard introduces multiple possible paths, which are explored using recursion and backtracking.

This approach is particularly useful for implementing dictionaries with wildcard character matching.
